      "id": 1,
      "text": "Smoot was first known as Cottonwood and later Belview before being renamed to honor Senator Reed Smoot of Utah.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.starvalleyhistoricalsociety.org/Smoot/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The settlement of Smoot dates back to 1886 when the first colonists established their homes here.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.starvalleyhistoricalsociety.org/Smoot/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Smoot's first school opened around 1890 in a small log room with desks made from wood slabs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.starvalleyhistoricalsociety.org/Smoot/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Cottonwood Lake was created for irrigation and later became a summer resort featuring an open-air dance hall.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.starvalleyhistoricalsociety.org/Smoot/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Electricity first arrived in the community of Smoot in 1938 through Lower Valley Power and Light.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.starvalleyhistoricalsociety.org/Smoot/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Salt River Pass rises out of Smoot to the south, reaching a maximum elevation of 7,630 feet.",
